{
  "id": "l11",
  "number": 11,
  "chapter": 2,
  "title": "The Present Participle",
  "subtitle": "The man who is cooking · -nta / -māna · masculine and neuter",
  "tags": [
    "Verb form",
    "Participle",
    "Adjective"
  ],
  "minutes": 18,
  "revision": {
    "title": "Quick Revision — Lessons IX and X",
    "summaryHtml": "You now have three ways to use a verb: the <strong>finite verb</strong> (<em>gacchati</em>, “goes”), the <strong>gerund</strong> (<em>gantvā</em>, “having gone”) and the <strong>infinitive</strong> (<em>gantuṃ</em>, “to go”). Gerund and infinitive never change their endings.",
    "connectionHtml": "<strong>Connection:</strong> The present participle is different: it <em>does</em> change. It behaves like an adjective, describing a noun and matching its case and number: “the man <em>who is cooking</em>”.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "q": "Which form means “to go”?",
        "a": "<strong>gantuṃ</strong>"
      },
      {
        "q": "Which form means “having eaten”?",
        "a": "<strong>khāditvā</strong> (or bhutvā)"
      },
      {
        "q": "Is <em>-tuṃ</em> ever plural?",
        "a": "<strong>No</strong>, the infinitive is indeclinable."
      }
    ]
  },
  "teach": [
    {
      "id": "participle",
      "title": "The Present Participle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part A",
          "html": "A verb that <em>describes</em>"
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "The <strong>present participle</strong> says that someone is <em>in the middle of</em> an action: “cooking”, “going”, “sitting”. It is formed from the verb’s <strong>base</strong> and works as an <strong>adjective</strong>."
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Forming the present participle",
          "formulas": [
            "base + <em>-nta</em> or <em>-māna</em> <span style='opacity:.4'>|</span> paca + nta = <em>pacanta</em> / pacamāna (cooking)",
            "gaccha + nta = <em>gacchanta</em> / gacchamāna (going)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Common present participles",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "Participle",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "pacati",
              "paca<span class=\"decl-ending\">nta</span> / paca<span class=\"decl-ending\">māna</span>",
              "cooking"
            ],
            [
              "gacchati",
              "gaccha<span class=\"decl-ending\">nta</span> / gaccha<span class=\"decl-ending\">māna</span>",
              "going"
            ],
            [
              "bhuñjati",
              "bhuñja<span class=\"decl-ending\">nta</span> / bhuñja<span class=\"decl-ending\">māna</span>",
              "eating"
            ],
            [
              "tiṭṭhati",
              "tiṭṭha<span class=\"decl-ending\">nta</span> / tiṭṭha<span class=\"decl-ending\">māna</span>",
              "standing"
            ],
            [
              "viharati",
              "vihara<span class=\"decl-ending\">nta</span> / vihara<span class=\"decl-ending\">māna</span>",
              "dwelling"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "html": "Because it is an adjective, the participle must <strong>agree</strong> with its noun in <strong>gender, number and case</strong>. Masculine and neuter forms decline just like the <em>-a</em> nouns you already know."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Grammar — Part B",
          "html": "Agreeing with the <em>noun</em>"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "pacanta / pacamāna (masculine) — cooking",
          "head": [
            "Case",
            "Singular",
            "Plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nominative",
              "pacan<span class=\"decl-ending\">to</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">o</span>",
              "pacant<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">ā</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Accusative",
              "pacant<span class=\"decl-ending\">aṃ</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">aṃ</span>",
              "pacant<span class=\"decl-ending\">e</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">e</span>"
            ],
            [
              "Instrumental",
              "pacant<span class=\"decl-ending\">ena</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">ena</span>",
              "pacant<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span> / pacamān<span class=\"decl-ending\">ehi</span>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "html": "The other cases follow the <em>-a</em> pattern from Chapter 1 (for example genitive <em>pacamānassa</em>). Neuter nouns use the Nominative/Accusative form <em>-aṃ</em>, as with <em>phalaṃ</em>."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "eyebrow": "Sentence building",
          "html": "Participle phrase + <em>noun</em>"
        },
        {
          "type": "rule",
          "title": "Pattern",
          "formulas": [
            "[object + <em>participle</em>] + noun — all in the same case as the noun",
            "Vejjo <span class=\"pali\">bhattaṃ pacantaṃ purisaṃ</span> passati."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "html": "<em>Vejjo bhattaṃ pacantaṃ purisaṃ passati.</em> = “The doctor sees the man <strong>who is cooking rice</strong>.” <em>Pacantaṃ</em> and <em>purisaṃ</em> are both Accusative singular masculine, so they match."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 3,
          "html": "Common mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "mistakes",
          "items": [
            {
              "wrong": "Vejjo bhattaṃ pacanto purisaṃ passati.",
              "right": "Vejjo bhattaṃ pacantaṃ purisaṃ passati.",
              "why": "<em>Pacanto</em> is Nominative (the doctor!). To describe <em>purisaṃ</em> the participle must also be Accusative."
            },
            {
              "wrong": "Gacchanto kumārā hasanti.",
              "right": "Gacchantā kumārā hasanti.",
              "why": "Plural noun, plural participle: the ending must agree in number."
            },
            {
              "wrong": "Pacanti puriso hasati.",
              "right": "Pacanto puriso hasati.",
              "why": "Don’t confuse the verb ending <em>-nti</em> (“they cook”) with the participle <em>-nto</em> (“cooking”)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
